01:00:07,120 --> 01:00:09,100
And I was looking around for a hidden camera

6
01:00:09,100 --> 01:00:11,920
to see will an economist stoop down to pick

7
01:00:11,920 --> 01:00:13,400
up $20 from the sidewalk.

8
01:00:13,400 --> 01:00:14,920
And I did.

9
01:00:14,920 --> 01:00:15,700
And there was no camera.

10
01:00:15,700 --> 01:00:16,940
It just was, you know.

11
01:00:17,100 --> 01:00:18,960
How come I never found $20 when I was a kid?

12
01:00:18,960 --> 01:00:21,160
I guess there was inflation, but still.

13
01:00:21,160 --> 01:00:22,780
Like when I was a kid, I never found more than a dollar.

14
01:00:23,360 --> 01:00:25,120
So, yes. And that was when I needed the money.

15
01:00:25,120 --> 01:00:27,320
And, yes, there were liquidity constraints, although.

16
01:00:27,720 --> 01:00:29,180
Yes, I also had debt aversion when I was a kid.

17
01:00:29,180 --> 01:00:30,600
Or, you know, something more extreme.

18
01:00:30,600 --> 01:00:32,360
I had just poverty aversion.

19
01:00:32,360 --> 01:00:35,180
I just wanted to have a high visible net worth.

20
01:00:35,180 --> 01:00:37,920
So, I just would accumulate money and piggy bank.

21
01:00:37,920 --> 01:00:39,980
And say, once I wanted a checking account,

22
01:00:39,980 --> 01:00:40,620
put in the checking account.

23
01:00:40,620 --> 01:00:42,700
I just sat on it, building it up and feeling better

24
01:00:42,700 --> 01:00:44,160
than other people that I didn't spend money.

25
01:00:44,860 --> 01:00:46,740
Right? And if someone had said, look, you're being an idiot,

26
01:00:46,740 --> 01:00:48,520
Brian. You're going to be richer in the future.

27
01:00:48,520 --> 01:00:49,860
So, now is the time you should be spending.

28
01:00:49,860 --> 01:00:50,920
You shouldn't have money right now.

29
01:00:51,720 --> 01:00:53,560
Yes. If I, could I talk myself into it?

30
01:00:54,340 --> 01:00:56,080
Maybe. Yes.

31
01:00:56,540 --> 01:00:57,500
If I appeal to my ego.

32
01:00:57,500 --> 01:01:00,640
So, you know, you really, what I, you know,

33
01:01:00,640 --> 01:01:02,700
probably my brother always spent whatever money he got.

34
01:01:02,700 --> 01:01:04,580
Which turns out was actually the more sensible thing.

35
01:01:05,360 --> 01:01:07,740
Although, not given what he was spending the money on.

36
01:01:07,740 --> 01:01:10,660
Usually like bad business ideas and things like that.

37
01:01:10,660 --> 01:01:11,660
But, all right.

38
01:01:12,480 --> 01:01:15,680
Any case. So, rule of certainty, the price of every asset has

39
01:01:15,680 --> 01:01:17,260
to equal its present discounted value.

40
01:01:17,720 --> 01:01:19,300
And if you've got risk neutrality,

41
01:01:19,760 --> 01:01:21,980
so everyone is risk neutral, then this holds

42
01:01:21,980 --> 01:01:23,040
under uncertainty as well.

43
01:01:23,920 --> 01:01:25,420
Because in that case, everything will sell

44
01:01:25,420 --> 01:01:27,960
for its expected present discounted value.

45
01:01:27,960 --> 01:01:30,280
So, if you've got uncertainty, but people don't,

46
01:01:30,280 --> 01:01:32,720
but people value everything at the expected value.

47
01:01:33,580 --> 01:01:35,360
Right then, of course, if there's something

48
01:01:35,360 --> 01:01:38,280
with an expected value of $100 a year from now, and it goes

49
01:01:38,280 --> 01:01:41,240
for $90 right now, then that is an expected interest rate

50
01:01:41,240 --> 01:01:43,680
of 11%. And if people are risk neutral,

51
01:01:44,000 --> 01:01:46,880
then they pick whatever has the highest expected return,

52
01:01:46,880 --> 01:01:48,160
regardless of the variance.

53
01:01:48,620 --> 01:01:50,340
Right? That's the definition of risk neutrality.

54
01:01:50,820 --> 01:01:52,400
Right? Sound good?

55
01:01:53,060 --> 01:01:53,300
All right.

56
01:01:53,300 --> 01:01:56,720
Now, furthermore, imagine that everyone

57
01:01:56,720 --> 01:01:58,240
in the world is risk averse except for one person.

58
01:01:59,500 --> 01:02:01,260
In that case, will things still sell

59
01:02:01,260 --> 01:02:03,560
for their expected present discounted value?

60
01:02:05,660 --> 01:02:07,560
Sure, because that one person isn't going

61
01:02:07,560 --> 01:02:10,180
to have any effect upon world interest rates, are they?

62
01:02:11,040 --> 01:02:14,600
So, in that case, that one person who is risk averse will

63
01:02:14,600 --> 01:02:16,400
just sell off all the risky assets

64
01:02:16,400 --> 01:02:17,480
to the risk neutral people.

65
01:02:18,020 --> 01:02:20,400
And that one person isn't going to change the world market.

66
01:02:20,400 --> 01:02:23,240
So, in that case, that one person can have free insurance

67
01:02:23,560 --> 01:02:24,920
if everyone else is risk neutral.

68
01:02:24,920 --> 01:02:26,920
So, if you're the only risk averse person in the world,

69
01:02:26,920 --> 01:02:29,920
you can get insurance for free, essentially.

70
01:02:30,540 --> 01:02:33,200
But once those risk averse people become a significant part

71
01:02:33,200 --> 01:02:35,600
of the market, then they may have

72
01:02:35,600 --> 01:02:37,780
to start paying people premium in order to get them

73
01:02:37,780 --> 01:02:39,140
to provide the service.

74
01:02:40,320 --> 01:02:43,760
Okay. So, once there's enough risk averse agents,

75
01:02:43,760 --> 01:02:47,360
then assets, then factors besides present discounted value

76
01:02:47,360 --> 01:02:48,100
will start to matter.

77
01:02:48,100 --> 01:02:49,480
So, in particular, we should expect

78
01:02:49,480 --> 01:02:52,280
that assets will start trading for the present discounted value

79
01:02:53,240 --> 01:02:55,240
minus some risk discount.

80
01:02:59,240 --> 01:02:59,740
All right.

81
01:03:00,240 --> 01:03:01,560
And you're saying, but wait a second.

82
01:03:02,240 --> 01:03:04,320
Things trade for the present discounted value minus risk

83
01:03:04,320 --> 01:03:07,200
discount, but shouldn't you get a higher rate of return

84
01:03:07,200 --> 01:03:08,140
for the risky asset?

85
01:03:08,680 --> 01:03:10,240
And the answer is absolutely.

86
01:03:10,560 --> 01:03:12,440
And that's implied by this, right?

87
01:03:12,440 --> 01:03:17,160
Because when asset price falls, what by definition happens

88
01:03:17,160 --> 01:03:20,080
to the interest rate or the rate of return on the asset?

89
01:03:20,220 --> 01:03:21,220
Rises. Rises.

90
01:03:21,700 --> 01:03:23,960
So, not only is there no contradiction,

91
01:03:23,960 --> 01:03:25,860
it would be a contradiction if it didn't work that way.

92
01:03:26,420 --> 01:03:28,840
So, either, so when you're talking about the price,

93
01:03:30,280 --> 01:03:32,500
then you should expect something like that.

94
01:03:32,500 --> 01:03:34,080
And you're talking about the rate,

95
01:03:34,760 --> 01:03:38,120
then it should be normal,

96
01:03:38,120 --> 01:03:41,240
the normal rate plus a risk premium.

97
01:03:42,380 --> 01:03:44,740
So, how could things earn both a risk discount

98
01:03:44,740 --> 01:03:45,680
and a risk premium?

99
01:03:45,680 --> 01:03:48,300
Well, there's a risk discount on the price and a risk premium

100
01:03:48,300 --> 01:03:50,560
on the rate, and the discount

101
01:03:50,560 --> 01:03:53,000
and the premium are two sides of the same coin.

102
01:03:54,020 --> 01:03:55,480
Just like low interest rates

103
01:03:55,480 --> 01:03:57,820
and high bond prices are two sides of the same coin.

104
01:03:58,960 --> 01:04:01,540
So, never say there's a correlation between the two.

105
01:04:01,540 --> 01:04:02,780
It's more than a correlation.

106
01:04:03,340 --> 01:04:04,140
It's an identity.

107
01:04:04,960 --> 01:04:07,420
Right? So, important to realize.

108
01:04:09,420 --> 01:04:11,360
Okay. All right.

109
01:04:11,800 --> 01:04:13,760
Now, this is complicated by the fact

110
01:04:13,760 --> 01:04:16,220
that there's actually a ton of different risky assets.

111
01:04:17,040 --> 01:04:19,960
Right? And there's a basic theorem of probability.

112
01:04:19,960 --> 01:04:21,120
Remember the law of large numbers?

113
01:04:21,880 --> 01:04:23,240
So, the law of large numbers tells us

114
01:04:23,240 --> 01:04:26,640
that the average riskiness of a bundle of risks is less

115
01:04:26,640 --> 01:04:27,780
than the average riskiness

116
01:04:27,780 --> 01:04:29,340
of an equal dollar amount of the same risk.

117
01:04:29,960 --> 01:04:31,360
So, if there are 10 assets,

118
01:04:31,360 --> 01:04:33,080
and they all have the same standard deviation

119
01:04:33,080 --> 01:04:37,880
on the return, still, if you put 100% of your money into one

120
01:04:37,880 --> 01:04:41,200
of those 10 assets, you will have a higher amount of risk

121
01:04:41,200 --> 01:04:44,420
than if you put 10% of your money into the 10 assets.

122
01:04:45,880 --> 01:04:47,700
Right? And this can be true even if each one

123
01:04:47,700 --> 01:04:51,240
of them has the same variance, same standard deviation.

124
01:04:52,060 --> 01:04:54,760
As long as those risks are not perfectly correlated,

125
01:04:55,100 --> 01:04:57,180
by diversifying your money,

126
01:04:57,240 --> 01:05:00,580
you can reduce risk for free in a way.

127
01:05:01,780 --> 01:05:01,980
All right?

128
01:05:02,480 --> 01:05:05,680
What this means, then, is that it is possible to a degree

129
01:05:06,080 --> 01:05:08,120
to do what we call diversify away risk.

130
01:05:08,120 --> 01:05:10,860
So, risk can be diversified away.

131
01:05:14,200 --> 01:05:18,780
Right? In an amazing world where there were some assets

132
01:05:18,780 --> 01:05:20,640
where they were perfectly negatively correlated

133
01:05:20,640 --> 01:05:23,940
with each other, you just put half your money in one asset,

134
01:05:24,280 --> 01:05:25,880
half your money in the negatively correlated,

135
01:05:25,880 --> 01:05:27,520
the perfectly negatively correlated asset,

136
01:05:27,800 --> 01:05:28,920
and you would have an income stream

137
01:05:28,920 --> 01:05:29,720
that was perfectly certain.

138
01:05:31,000 --> 01:05:33,200
So, that theoretically is possible.

139
01:05:33,620 --> 01:05:36,320
That even though all the, both assets are risky,

140
01:05:36,320 --> 01:05:38,440
the combination doesn't have to be risky at all.

141
01:05:39,320 --> 01:05:39,760
Strangely.

142
01:05:41,100 --> 01:05:41,320
All right.

143
01:05:41,960 --> 01:05:45,060
And what this means is insofar as you can diversify

144
01:05:45,060 --> 01:05:46,940
that risk away, you shouldn't expect

145
01:05:46,940 --> 01:05:48,100
that stuff to earn the premium.

146
01:05:49,200 --> 01:05:50,280
So, that kind of risk,

147
01:05:50,280 --> 01:05:53,580
risk that you can actually weasel your way out of.

148
01:05:54,000 --> 01:05:56,060
Risk that you can actually just repack it and say,

149
01:05:56,060 --> 01:05:56,660
oh, it's risky.

150
01:05:56,660 --> 01:05:58,300
You know, like, rearrange things.

151
01:05:58,300 --> 01:06:00,960
You know, sort of like take a transformer, move the wings.

152
01:06:01,200 --> 01:06:01,520
All right?

153
01:06:01,860 --> 01:06:02,960
I'm not risky anymore.

154
01:06:02,960 --> 01:06:05,980
I turned this truck into a samurai.

155
01:06:06,560 --> 01:06:06,880
All right?

156
01:06:06,880 --> 01:06:09,000
So, similarly, I turned a bunch of risky assets

157
01:06:09,000 --> 01:06:13,080
into one perfect solid block of non-risky assets.

158
01:06:13,540 --> 01:06:14,280
Isn't that amazing?

159
01:06:15,500 --> 01:06:18,080
Okay. So, then what are you supposed

160
01:06:18,080 --> 01:06:19,780
to earn this risk premium for?

161
01:06:20,140 --> 01:06:22,420
And the answer is something special.

162
01:06:22,420 --> 01:06:24,000
It's undiversifiable risk.

163
01:06:26,500 --> 01:06:30,680
So, undiversifiable risk is actually the relevant risk

164
01:06:30,680 --> 01:06:33,700
that causes a discount in prices,

165
01:06:34,040 --> 01:06:35,800
causes a premium in returns.

166
01:06:37,020 --> 01:06:38,960
Okay. All right.

167
01:06:39,720 --> 01:06:41,140
So, what does this mean?

168
01:06:41,740 --> 01:06:45,380
Well, so, the usual way that people actually think

169
01:06:45,380 --> 01:06:51,280
about this is first you imagine constructing a market portfolio

170
01:06:51,900 --> 01:06:54,960
where you have an optimized portfolio of all the assets

171
01:06:54,960 --> 01:06:57,760
that exist, and you see how low you can get the variance

172
01:06:57,760 --> 01:06:58,240
on that down.

173
01:06:58,240 --> 01:07:01,460
And in the real world, it still has a lot of variance.

174
01:07:02,400 --> 01:07:06,700
So, in the real world, if you go and you try to figure out,

175
01:07:06,700 --> 01:07:08,660
so what is the lowest variance portfolio I can get

176
01:07:08,660 --> 01:07:09,940
that has a given rate of return?

177
01:07:10,400 --> 01:07:12,160
Well, you know, like almost never can you get it

178
01:07:12,160 --> 01:07:14,120
down to zero unless you accept a really low rate.

179
01:07:14,880 --> 01:07:16,920
And even that isn't really zero variance, right?

180
01:07:16,920 --> 01:07:18,420
Even if you put it all into government bonds.

181
01:07:19,040 --> 01:07:20,700
What are the risks of government bonds?

182
01:07:21,480 --> 01:07:23,880
Well, there's the blatant risk.

183
01:07:23,880 --> 01:07:26,180
There's the risk that we're always facing,

184
01:07:26,180 --> 01:07:29,220
and then there's the long shot risk that, nevertheless,

185
01:07:29,220 --> 01:07:30,140
has burned a lot of people.

186
01:07:30,880 --> 01:07:33,100
So, what's the obvious risk of a regular government bond?

187
01:07:34,560 --> 01:07:34,920
What's that?

188
01:07:35,100 --> 01:07:35,760
Inflation.

189
01:07:35,800 --> 01:07:36,380
Inflation, yeah.

190
01:07:36,380 --> 01:07:37,620
Inflation is the obvious risk.

191
01:07:38,040 --> 01:07:41,060
And then what is the long shot risk

192
01:07:41,060 --> 01:07:42,720
that has burned lots of people throughout history?

193
01:07:42,940 --> 01:07:44,520
Default. Default, yes.

194
01:07:45,060 --> 01:07:48,240
So, everyone who owns Zara's debt lost everything,

195
01:07:48,980 --> 01:07:49,520
for example.

196
01:07:50,360 --> 01:07:50,620
All right.

197
01:07:51,300 --> 01:07:53,020
So, when there's even been some effort to try

198
01:07:53,020 --> 01:07:55,660
to explain anomalies in financial markets

199
01:07:55,660 --> 01:07:58,160
by looking at the small risks of total wipeout,

200
01:07:58,720 --> 01:08:00,060
which do occasionally occur.

201
01:08:01,300 --> 01:08:01,500
All right.

202
01:08:01,500 --> 01:08:05,540
The usual punchline explains a bit, but not nearly everything.

203
01:08:06,360 --> 01:08:07,720
Okay. So, anyway, so we first go

204
01:08:07,720 --> 01:08:10,320
and we find this optimized bundle of market assets,

205
01:08:10,680 --> 01:08:12,800
and then we go and we can look at the correlation

206
01:08:12,800 --> 01:08:14,540
of individual assets with that market bundle.

207
01:08:15,320 --> 01:08:20,000
Right? And insofar as assets correlate with the movements

208
01:08:20,000 --> 01:08:22,140
of a given asset correlates with that market bundle,

209
01:08:22,480 --> 01:08:24,960
you can say each asset is actually going to go

210
01:08:24,960 --> 01:08:27,620
and earn some risk premium because you haven't been able

211
01:08:27,620 --> 01:08:28,720
to diversify away that risk.

212
01:08:29,760 --> 01:08:30,720
Right? On the other hand,

213
01:08:30,720 --> 01:08:32,840
if you actually found something that negatively correlated

214
01:08:32,840 --> 01:08:37,120
with the market, then that asset should actually have a risk.

215
01:08:37,120 --> 01:08:40,980
It should actually pay a risk, you know, it should actually pay

216
01:08:40,980 --> 01:08:43,220
more than an asset of certain value

217
01:08:44,300 --> 01:08:46,440
because you can actually take that asset, combine it

218
01:08:46,440 --> 01:08:49,100
with the risky stuff, and get rid of that uncertainty.

219
01:08:50,060 --> 01:08:53,760
So, my favorite example of this is on the first day

220
01:08:53,760 --> 01:08:56,300
of the really huge stock market crash in 2008.

221
01:08:56,300 --> 01:08:58,640
I think there were like five days of horrible crashes.

222
01:08:59,080 --> 01:09:01,920
But on the first day, out of the S&P 500,

223
01:09:01,920 --> 01:09:03,840
there was only one stock that went up in value.

224
01:09:05,020 --> 01:09:06,540
What company went up in value the day

225
01:09:06,540 --> 01:09:08,660
that the other 499 went down?

226
01:09:11,380 --> 01:09:12,160
Campbell Soup.

227
01:09:14,300 --> 01:09:17,980
So, maybe it's just coincidence, but, whoa.

228
01:09:18,300 --> 01:09:18,700
All right.

229
01:09:19,040 --> 01:09:21,640
So, when the rate position is kicking in, Campbell Soup goes

230
01:09:21,640 --> 01:09:24,880
up, well, it seems plausible that a lot of people

231
01:09:24,880 --> 01:09:26,760
who would not normally be eating Campbell Soup are going

232
01:09:26,760 --> 01:09:28,600
to, planning on, I guess it's going to be a few years

233
01:09:28,600 --> 01:09:29,620
of eating Campbell Soup now.

234
01:09:30,340 --> 01:09:33,260
So, anyway, that was the one stock that went up,

235
01:09:33,260 --> 01:09:33,920
at least that one day.

236
01:09:33,920 --> 01:09:35,000
I don't know what it did the other day.

237
01:09:35,000 --> 01:09:37,640
So, that is at least one plausible answer.

238
01:09:38,080 --> 01:09:40,560
Some people have talked about, like, the resume business

239
01:09:40,560 --> 01:09:43,440
as maybe being, as having a negative correlation

240
01:09:43,440 --> 01:09:44,240
with the stock market.

241
01:09:44,240 --> 01:09:48,600
And I think Keynes had the idea, if only we'd be on some kind

242
01:09:48,600 --> 01:09:49,940
of like a clay brick standard,

243
01:09:49,940 --> 01:09:52,700
then every time there's a recession, there'd be a boom

244
01:09:52,700 --> 01:09:53,680
in the clay brick industry.

245
01:09:54,240 --> 01:09:55,620
Because, you know, it's like a cost of return

246
01:09:55,620 --> 01:09:57,940
to scale industry, you just go and make clay bricks and use,

247
01:09:57,940 --> 01:10:00,760
and you turn them in and get paper certificates as money,

248
01:10:01,200 --> 01:10:01,800
something like that.

249
01:10:02,700 --> 01:10:05,240
Okay. So, anyway, if there were something negatively correlated

250
01:10:05,240 --> 01:10:06,280
with the average market rate,

251
01:10:06,720 --> 01:10:08,500
then it would actually be more valuable

252
01:10:08,500 --> 01:10:09,880
than a truly riskless asset.

253
01:10:12,020 --> 01:10:12,280
All right.

254
01:10:12,600 --> 01:10:14,420
So, questions about that?

255
01:10:15,000 --> 01:10:15,720
Questions?

256
01:10:16,200 --> 01:10:16,660
All right.

257
01:10:17,340 --> 01:10:19,360
So, now, yeah, we've got time to do this.

258
01:10:19,360 --> 01:10:19,900
All right.

259
01:10:19,900 --> 01:10:20,920
So, mean variance efficiency.

260
01:10:21,780 --> 01:10:26,420
So, a popular simplifying assumption in finance is

261
01:10:26,420 --> 01:10:29,700
that people only care about two features of their portfolios.

262
01:10:29,700 --> 01:10:30,740
Let's see.

263
01:10:30,740 --> 01:10:32,460
Wait. Am I totally out of?

264
01:10:33,860 --> 01:10:35,840
No. I guess this is what people are using.

265
01:10:36,280 --> 01:10:36,480
All right.

266
01:10:37,960 --> 01:10:40,980
There was a half-destroyed eraser last week,

267
01:10:40,980 --> 01:10:43,260
but it was still almost as good as a regular eraser.

