01:44:57,000 --> 01:44:58,820
because then you'd have a zero multiplied in there,

121
01:44:58,820 --> 01:45:01,460
and then it would fit with the fact that you own nothing.

122
01:45:01,580 --> 01:45:04,240
But yes. All right.

123
01:45:04,240 --> 01:45:07,960
So anyway, there's a bunch of, but there are a bunch

124
01:45:07,960 --> 01:45:10,580
of anomalies for the MH, and some of the ones

125
01:45:10,580 --> 01:45:12,060
that have gotten the most attention are called

126
01:45:12,060 --> 01:45:12,880
calendar effects.

127
01:45:14,800 --> 01:45:16,040
So calendar effects.

128
01:45:16,540 --> 01:45:18,220
Excuse me.

129
01:45:20,660 --> 01:45:24,160
Effects. And the best note

130
01:45:24,160 --> 01:45:27,200
of all these is the legendary January effect.

131
01:45:28,100 --> 01:45:28,980
The January effect.

132
01:45:28,980 --> 01:45:35,300
So, Thaler Reports, book published, I think,

133
01:45:35,300 --> 01:45:37,040
like around 99 or so.

134
01:45:37,220 --> 01:45:39,660
I don't remember when it was, when it was first published.

135
01:45:39,660 --> 01:45:41,720
So thereabouts, I think.

136
01:45:42,300 --> 01:45:44,400
So back then, he predicted, he said that, you know,

137
01:45:44,400 --> 01:45:46,600
average New York Stock Exchange monthly returns from February

138
01:45:46,600 --> 01:45:48,560
to December are.5% per month.

139
01:45:49,360 --> 01:45:51,220
Average January return is 3.5%.

140
01:45:51,940 --> 01:45:55,120
So there's one month that has seven times the normal rate

141
01:45:55,120 --> 01:45:55,520
of return.

142
01:45:55,520 --> 01:45:57,440
All right.

143
01:45:57,840 --> 01:46:01,480
And if you look at it further, it seems like it primarily comes

144
01:46:01,480 --> 01:46:05,420
from these really high returns for small firms in January.

145
01:46:06,960 --> 01:46:09,940
So you just buy, invest in the small firms in January,

146
01:46:09,940 --> 01:46:13,960
and you get over half your annual return right then.

147
01:46:13,960 --> 01:46:14,540
I think, let's see.

148
01:46:14,540 --> 01:46:15,100
Or no, no, no.

149
01:46:15,100 --> 01:46:15,440
Let's see.

150
01:46:15,440 --> 01:46:16,140
Would it be over half?

151
01:46:16,140 --> 01:46:17,580
No, it wouldn't be quite that good.

152
01:46:17,580 --> 01:46:17,900
Let's see.

153
01:46:17,900 --> 01:46:20,720
Eleven. So, you know, you got like two-thirds

154
01:46:20,720 --> 01:46:21,400
of your annual return.

155
01:46:21,400 --> 01:46:23,440
Or like one-third of your annual return, but still pretty good.

156
01:46:23,440 --> 01:46:24,820
All right.

157
01:46:24,820 --> 01:46:26,980
And then you might think this is just a U.S. specific thing.

158
01:46:27,420 --> 01:46:29,440
So, of course, this inspired a lot of research.

159
01:46:29,440 --> 01:46:32,060
And 15 out of 16 countries studied had a January effect

160
01:46:32,520 --> 01:46:33,240
by the late 90s.

161
01:46:35,740 --> 01:46:36,740
All right.

162
01:46:36,740 --> 01:46:40,880
Now, since then, many people have, you know,

163
01:46:40,880 --> 01:46:42,880
people working in this area have generally said the January

164
01:46:42,880 --> 01:46:45,420
effect has either gone away or is going away.

165
01:46:46,080 --> 01:46:47,120
So I haven't kept up to date

166
01:46:47,120 --> 01:46:48,900
on what the very latest word on it is.

167
01:46:49,460 --> 01:46:52,400
But a common saying is the January effect is now happening

168
01:46:52,400 --> 01:46:53,560
in December.

169
01:46:54,260 --> 01:46:55,200
Right? And that's the idea.

170
01:46:55,200 --> 01:46:57,560
Well, if everybody knows that it's going to go up in January,

171
01:46:57,560 --> 01:46:58,540
then it won't go up in January.

172
01:46:58,540 --> 01:46:59,340
It'll go up in December.

173
01:46:59,900 --> 01:47:02,860
But I think actually there's just been a general annual,

174
01:47:02,860 --> 01:47:05,540
general smoothing over calendar months.

175
01:47:05,540 --> 01:47:07,640
But I haven't followed that closely.

176
01:47:07,640 --> 01:47:09,880
So I'm not sure what the current story is.

177
01:47:10,300 --> 01:47:11,760
But even if it goes away totally,

178
01:47:11,760 --> 01:47:15,260
the fact that it was big for many decades and 15

179
01:47:15,260 --> 01:47:19,260
out of 16 countries studied is not the kind of thing you can

180
01:47:19,260 --> 01:47:20,440
just say, ah, it doesn't mean anything.

181
01:47:20,440 --> 01:47:22,660
It's a split.

182
01:47:22,660 --> 01:47:23,740
So that's one case.

183
01:47:24,680 --> 01:47:27,860
Another one that is not nearly as impressive

184
01:47:27,860 --> 01:47:30,320
but still well-documented is something called the

185
01:47:30,320 --> 01:47:30,960
weekend effect.

186
01:47:31,460 --> 01:47:33,740
So when you go and leave your money in a bank account

187
01:47:33,740 --> 01:47:36,360
with continuous compounding over the weekend,

188
01:47:36,840 --> 01:47:39,880
how much interest do you get between Friday and Monday?

189
01:47:43,300 --> 01:47:44,300
So continuous compounding.

190
01:47:45,000 --> 01:47:48,940
So between Friday morning and Monday morning,

191
01:47:48,940 --> 01:47:50,540
how many days have passed?

192
01:47:51,320 --> 01:47:52,300
Three days.

193
01:47:52,820 --> 01:47:53,840
So with continuous compounding,

194
01:47:53,840 --> 01:47:54,820
how many days interest do you get?

195
01:47:55,460 --> 01:47:56,560
Three. All right.

196
01:47:56,560 --> 01:47:57,480
So pretty obvious.

197
01:47:57,980 --> 01:47:58,260
All right.

198
01:47:58,260 --> 01:48:01,300
Now it would seem like stock markets would also have

199
01:48:01,300 --> 01:48:02,500
continuous compounding, right?

200
01:48:02,500 --> 01:48:06,460
So people are always able to go and buy and sell.

201
01:48:06,460 --> 01:48:14,640
So anyway, people could go and dump their portfolio on Friday,

202
01:48:14,840 --> 01:48:16,420
buy something with continuous compounding,

203
01:48:16,420 --> 01:48:19,380
and then sell the continuously compounded thing on Monday

204
01:48:19,380 --> 01:48:22,040
morning and then buy back their portfolio

205
01:48:22,040 --> 01:48:24,420
if they weren't getting the continuously compounded return

206
01:48:24,420 --> 01:48:25,180
on the stocks.

207
01:48:25,820 --> 01:48:27,880
So it seems like the average Friday

208
01:48:27,880 --> 01:48:29,660
and Monday return should be three times the normal return.

209
01:48:30,820 --> 01:48:32,420
Right? But actually,

210
01:48:32,420 --> 01:48:34,240
Monday returns don't seem to be especially good.

211
01:48:35,380 --> 01:48:36,900
So that's another thing that's been found.

212
01:48:38,560 --> 01:48:42,120
Now, Thaler, who again is, if you go and read him carefully,

213
01:48:42,120 --> 01:48:43,980
he's always more reasonable than the summary.

214
01:48:43,980 --> 01:48:45,440
All right.

215
01:48:45,440 --> 01:48:46,640
So, all right.

216
01:48:46,640 --> 01:48:49,660
And possibly more reasonable than his own summary when he's

217
01:48:49,660 --> 01:48:50,940
on TV or something like that.

218
01:48:50,940 --> 01:48:54,260
So, you know, I have been on TV, so yes,

219
01:48:54,260 --> 01:48:56,020
you do have to simplify, but still.

220
01:48:56,420 --> 01:48:59,620
If you do read him, he says something that is much richer

221
01:48:59,620 --> 01:49:01,200
than the view that is normally attributed to him.

222
01:49:01,820 --> 01:49:03,940
So one thing he admits is, look, sure,

223
01:49:03,940 --> 01:49:05,760
these anomalies are pretty hard to take advantage

224
01:49:05,760 --> 01:49:07,020
of due to transactions costs.

225
01:49:08,320 --> 01:49:11,380
But what he says is, you know, at least you should expect people

226
01:49:11,380 --> 01:49:13,240
to adjust the timing of the transactions.

227
01:49:13,240 --> 01:49:15,360
In order to take advantage of the pattern.

228
01:49:15,480 --> 01:49:17,560
So if you know that you're going to buy stocks once a year,

229
01:49:17,560 --> 01:49:19,700
there's a January effect, when should you buy the stocks?

230
01:49:21,240 --> 01:49:21,900
By the end of December.

231
01:49:22,360 --> 01:49:23,640
Right? And then either way,

232
01:49:23,640 --> 01:49:24,880
you're paying the transactions costs.

233
01:49:24,880 --> 01:49:26,140
But then in one case, you're going

234
01:49:26,140 --> 01:49:27,100
and getting your best deal.

235
01:49:27,480 --> 01:49:28,600
Whereas if you just do it randomly,

236
01:49:28,600 --> 01:49:30,220
maybe the dice will come out February.

237
01:49:30,220 --> 01:49:32,000
And then you're getting a way lower return.

238
01:49:32,400 --> 01:49:33,880
And you pay the same transactions costs.

239
01:49:36,520 --> 01:49:36,860
All right.

240
01:49:36,860 --> 01:49:39,580
And yes, so similarly, so there would be a problem

241
01:49:39,580 --> 01:49:42,700
in the real world in selling all your stocks on Friday morning

242
01:49:42,700 --> 01:49:44,660
and then buying them back on Monday morning.

243
01:49:44,720 --> 01:49:46,660
Or Friday evening and then buying them back Monday morning.

244
01:49:47,060 --> 01:49:48,960
Which is almost every place would go and charge a fee,

245
01:49:48,960 --> 01:49:50,080
which would wipe out the benefits.

246
01:49:50,800 --> 01:49:52,260
You see, like, if you really know what you're doing,

247
01:49:52,260 --> 01:49:55,440
you might be able to have a, one of the brokerages

248
01:49:55,440 --> 01:49:58,740
where they just charge a flat amount per sale order

249
01:49:58,740 --> 01:49:59,820
or purchase order.

250
01:50:00,500 --> 01:50:03,200
And then if you, and then if you don't diversify as much,

251
01:50:03,200 --> 01:50:04,400
you might go, you might be able

252
01:50:04,400 --> 01:50:06,780
to get the transactions costs down.

253
01:50:07,180 --> 01:50:08,120
If you have an index fund,

254
01:50:08,120 --> 01:50:10,940
normally they actually will not allow you to do stuff like that.

255
01:50:10,940 --> 01:50:13,560
So normally for index funds, there's a rule saying

256
01:50:13,560 --> 01:50:15,400
that you're not allowed to buy or sell more often

257
01:50:15,440 --> 01:50:16,580
than every few months.

258
01:50:17,960 --> 01:50:19,440
And actually, when you call Redeem,

259
01:50:19,800 --> 01:50:23,140
the last few times I called, they said, why are you selling?

260
01:50:23,860 --> 01:50:26,060
And if you say, to market time, then they'll say, oh,

261
01:50:26,060 --> 01:50:28,180
I'm sorry, sir, you're not allowed to do that.

262
01:50:28,800 --> 01:50:30,880
So, or something I want to say, like,

263
01:50:30,880 --> 01:50:32,580
if there's an extra fee if you do that,

264
01:50:32,580 --> 01:50:34,780
we're saying just I need the money, then there's no fee.

265
01:50:35,460 --> 01:50:37,720
So it's a little bit funny as to how they enforce that.

266
01:50:38,260 --> 01:50:40,420
The fact they ask is just maybe sometimes people do

267
01:50:40,420 --> 01:50:42,600
to support it out, and then they can scold them at least.

268
01:50:44,600 --> 01:50:44,880
All right.

269
01:50:45,700 --> 01:50:49,680
Yes, and like another calendar effect, well, which, again,

270
01:50:50,620 --> 01:50:53,260
it's hard to believe, but I didn't, I met one

271
01:50:53,260 --> 01:50:55,480
of the primary researchers, and she really knew her stuff.

272
01:50:55,480 --> 01:50:57,720
So I will tell you what she told me.

273
01:50:58,300 --> 01:51:00,500
So there's a researcher who, like, has,

274
01:51:00,500 --> 01:51:03,720
there's a paper called, this is your portfolio on winter.

275
01:51:04,440 --> 01:51:06,640
And it's all about the calendar effect

276
01:51:06,640 --> 01:51:08,460
from seasonal affective disorder.

277
01:51:08,460 --> 01:51:11,740
Right, and the, so seasonal affective disorder,

278
01:51:11,740 --> 01:51:14,220
so there's something like 10 to 15%

279
01:51:14,220 --> 01:51:16,480
of people feel more depressed during winter.

280
01:51:17,860 --> 01:51:20,500
And the standard view is that it's just

281
01:51:20,500 --> 01:51:21,520
because they're getting less sunlight.

282
01:51:22,360 --> 01:51:24,300
So people get happier when there's sunlight,

283
01:51:24,300 --> 01:51:26,640
and most people don't really care particularly,

284
01:51:26,980 --> 01:51:29,940
but there is a segment of the population, like 10 to 15%,

285
01:51:30,260 --> 01:51:30,960
that gets really bummed

286
01:51:30,960 --> 01:51:32,020
out when they don't have enough sunlight.

287
01:51:33,020 --> 01:51:35,220
So anyway, what these researchers did is they actually

288
01:51:35,220 --> 01:51:38,540
went and tracked down, based upon your latitude,

289
01:51:38,900 --> 01:51:40,300
how much sunlight you're getting

290
01:51:40,300 --> 01:51:41,920
in all the different stock exchanges.

291
01:51:42,240 --> 01:51:44,260
And then they looked at what's going on for,

292
01:51:44,260 --> 01:51:48,580
either in the stock exchanges or in country, or in the firms

293
01:51:48,580 --> 01:51:50,580
that are based in the countries of the stock exchanges.

294
01:51:51,220 --> 01:51:53,520
And note, not only are you able to get variation

295
01:51:53,520 --> 01:51:56,340
from latitude, but remember how there's two hemispheres?

296
01:51:57,360 --> 01:52:01,160
So, and when the Arctic has full sunlight,

297
01:52:01,160 --> 01:52:02,140
what does the Antarctic have?

298
01:52:03,820 --> 01:52:04,240
Full night.

299
01:52:04,240 --> 01:52:07,420
So what this means is actually there's a reliable pattern

300
01:52:07,800 --> 01:52:11,160
of sunlight, and they claim that when you go

301
01:52:11,160 --> 01:52:14,040
and test global financial markets, a particular,

302
01:52:14,040 --> 01:52:17,200
like based upon each country or each stock exchange,

303
01:52:17,600 --> 01:52:19,880
for seasonal affective disorder based upon their latitude

304
01:52:19,880 --> 01:52:21,120
and the time of year,

305
01:52:21,620 --> 01:52:24,460
they claim that they actually get a very predictable pattern

306
01:52:24,460 --> 01:52:28,720
of returns get depressed during times of low sunlight.

307
01:52:28,720 --> 01:52:31,960
Or actually, I believe it's prices get depressed,

308
01:52:32,240 --> 01:52:33,300
which means the returns are high.

309
01:52:33,300 --> 01:52:34,900
Yes, that's the actual results.

310
01:52:34,900 --> 01:52:37,420
So people get depressed, they sell, oh man,

311
01:52:37,420 --> 01:52:39,980
the world's terrible, there's so little sunlight.

312
01:52:40,300 --> 01:52:42,640
And then there's enough people feel this way

313
01:52:42,640 --> 01:52:44,620
that the sales depress prices.

314
01:52:44,940 --> 01:52:47,260
And then if you don't sell your stuff during that time,

315
01:52:47,260 --> 01:52:48,260
you can make an extra return.

316
01:52:49,320 --> 01:52:50,560
And in principle, this does mean

317
01:52:50,560 --> 01:52:53,220
that if you could just oscillate between,

318
01:52:53,440 --> 01:52:55,040
like the Buenos Aires Stock Exchange

319
01:52:55,040 --> 01:52:58,240
or the Stockholm Stock Exchange, so you're always going

320
01:52:58,240 --> 01:53:02,400
and selling when there is, you want to sell

321
01:53:02,400 --> 01:53:04,160
when there's a lot of sunlight, I believe, and buy

322
01:53:04,160 --> 01:53:05,260
when there's very little sunlight,

323
01:53:05,700 --> 01:53:07,160
then you could make some extra return.

324
01:53:07,880 --> 01:53:09,780
So I mean, I'm sure I was asking, but like,

325
01:53:10,120 --> 01:53:11,360
that sounds really easy.

326
01:53:11,360 --> 01:53:12,240
Wouldn't everybody do it?

327
01:53:12,240 --> 01:53:13,460
I don't remember her answer to that.

328
01:53:13,540 --> 01:53:15,960
But this was a piece in the AR, so I'm sure,

329
01:53:15,960 --> 01:53:18,860
and it was controversial, so I'm sure the referees put it

330
01:53:18,860 --> 01:53:19,940
through a bunch of stress tests.

331
01:53:19,940 --> 01:53:20,200
Very good.

332
01:53:20,200 --> 01:53:20,460
All right.

333
01:53:20,460 --> 01:53:21,200
So, well, there are a few authors.

334
01:53:21,200 --> 01:53:24,360
So, but there's, but the piece, this is your brain on winter,

335
01:53:24,360 --> 01:53:25,420
or this is your portfolio?

336
01:53:25,420 --> 01:53:31,980
I remember it's investigating the relationship

337
01:53:31,980 --> 01:53:37,400
between the weather and the stock return in NYSE.

338
01:53:37,400 --> 01:53:39,140
So weather conditions.

339
01:53:39,140 --> 01:53:41,800
So weather, so but the weather or sunlight?

340
01:53:41,800 --> 01:53:42,780
So this one is about sunlight.

341
01:53:42,780 --> 01:53:46,080
Oh, he said about weather, maybe the sunny days,

342
01:53:46,080 --> 01:53:47,720
the stock return is high.

343
01:53:47,720 --> 01:53:51,260
Okay, so in, so they don't have, like in this paper,

344
01:53:51,260 --> 01:53:52,640
they don't have a lot of information.

345
01:53:52,640 --> 01:53:58,060
They specifically didn't actually look at local weather.

346
01:53:58,380 --> 01:54:00,580
They looked at the amount of sunlight that is coming

347
01:54:00,580 --> 01:54:02,660
to you from the earth, you know, like coming to you from the sun.

348
01:54:03,220 --> 01:54:05,100
So I mean, I'm saying, like, well, what about if there's,

349
01:54:05,100 --> 01:54:07,500
like, really thick thunderstorms or whatever?

350
01:54:07,880 --> 01:54:09,120
And, you know, they don't count that.

351
01:54:09,120 --> 01:54:12,380
So they base their measure purely upon your latitude

352
01:54:12,380 --> 01:54:14,480
and time of year and, of course, which hemisphere you're in.

353
01:54:15,940 --> 01:54:17,560
So anyway, there's another one.

354
01:54:18,740 --> 01:54:19,000
All right.

355
01:54:19,060 --> 01:54:20,920
So questions about calendar effects?

356
01:54:21,380 --> 01:54:21,660
I guess.

357
01:54:21,660 --> 01:54:22,860
There might be one more.

358
01:54:22,860 --> 01:54:25,580
I heard about it a little while ago, and I was just looking it

359
01:54:25,580 --> 01:54:28,220
up to see what sort of recent research might be on it.

360
01:54:28,220 --> 01:54:29,960
But there might be a congressional impact.

